<speak>That is entirely reasonable. We can avoid standing placeholders and make the format explicit when we arrange an appointment. If your week becomes crowded, an early message about availability is easier to manage than trying to catch you repeatedly during the day. There is no need to explain every business interruption. A simple note that you need another time is sufficient for routine scheduling. You mentioned travel when we arranged today's call. Is that likely to affect your availability for ordinary correspondence or follow-up conversations? We can keep any travel discussion at the level of timing and convenience.</speak>